In article <3544997.1067601360@dbforums.com>,
sung min cho  <member46343@dbforums.com> wrote:
> Is that possible, have 2 IP's on same NIC (Interface) ?

The option to ifconfig is alias, eg:

# ifconfig tu0 alias 192.168.0.101 netmask 255.255.255.0

To make it permanent... in the rc.config file, before:

NUM_NETCONFIG="2"
MAX_NETDEVS="24"
NETDEV_0="tu0"
NETDEV_1="tu1"
NETDEV_2=
IFCONFIG_0="10.0.0.150 netmask 255.255.255.0 speed 100 broadcast 10.0.0.255"
IFCONFIG_1="192.168.0.150 netmask 255.255.255.0 speed 200"
IFCONFIG_2=

After:

NUM_NETCONFIG="3"
MAX_NETDEVS="24"
NETDEV_0="tu0"
NETDEV_1="tu1"
NETDEV_2="tu1"
IFCONFIG_0="10.0.0.150 netmask 255.255.255.0 speed 100 broadcast 10.0.0.255"
IFCONFIG_1="192.168.0.150 netmask 255.255.255.0 speed 200"
IFCONFIG_2="alias 192.168.0.101 netmask 255.255.255.0"

