In article <DcVeb.3580$vO5.111800@twister1.libero.it>,
devnull <devnull@indirizzo.fasullo.it> wrote:
> what i'm doing is trying to write a script ("rc3script") into /sbin/init.d
> containing something like
> 
> su - user -c '$USERVARIABLECONTAININGAPATH/userscript.sh &'
> 
> when invoked with start as first parameter (yes it should be "forked").

First I'd make sure that it printed something at boot time outside of
this inner script:

echo "Starting userscript.sh as user from $USERVARIABLECONTAININGAPATH" \
	> /dev/console
su - user -c '$USERVARIABLECONTAININGAPATH/userscript.sh &'

That way you can see whether it's actually *trying* to run the script and
what it's running it as.

Also, you should call it like this:

nohup su - user -c 'whatever' < /dev/null > /dev/null 2>&1

Just to isolate it from potential signal problems.

