In news:3F7D8947.E4DB23A9@lorien.physics.louisville.edu,
Chris L.Davis <chris@lorien.physics.louisville.edu> typed:
> Don't know if this is the right newsgroup, but here goes...
> I have an old DEC alphastation 600 running digital Unix, whose
> internal disk is on the way out.  I have an external drive which
> is fine and I need to keep the system running for a few more
> months.  What I'd like to do is configure the external drive as a
> boot disk so that when the internal drive dies the machine will
> still be useable.  Can anyone point me towards instructions as to
>     how to accomplish this. Unfortunately I don't have any
> documentation on the machine and I am not a systems expert....
> Thanks
>
> Chris L. Davis

Here are some high level instructions, post back with questions about
the bits if you are having trouble.

Assuming that the external disk has no data on it that you want and
that there is no complicated stuff like LSM involved...

log in as root
Use disklabel -z to wipe the disklable on the disk.
Use the disklabel command to read make a label on the new
disk with partitions large enough to serve as replacements.
Make sure if you want to use advfs that you pass the -t advfs flag to disklabel.

Make the filesystems for / /usr and /var (Or make the domains/filesets).

Mount the new filesystems onto temporary mointpoints.

backup the data from / /usr and /var
recover the backups into the temporary mountpoints.

Edit the /etc/fstab in the new /etc to point to the external disk. (You may also need to change /etc/fdmns/* to have symbolic links
to the right places).

Shutdown to the >>> prompt.

Boot from the external disk using
boot DEV where DEV is the name of the external disk.

If it works shutdown and change the bootdef_dev to point to the external disk.
Boot again using the boot command (This time don't pass any arguments).
You have now moved the boot disk.

Remember that it is a high level overview of the steps and don't hesitate to
post more requests for help.
Hope this helps
Adam




